In article <31By02sv04XL01@JUTS.ccc.amdahl.com> jdg00@ccc.amdahl.com (Josh Grosse) writes:
   Wouldn't it be nice if one of the Nethack options was a random number
   seed?  This way if two people wanted to compete for points playing the
   exact same characters in the exact same dungeion -- it would add some
   competitiveness and be easier then a MUD version of nethack.  The "seeds"
   used could even be kept in the logfile to prevent practice and explore
   mode could be disallowed.  Cheaters will get around it anyway.

   (We might even see articles in r.g.h that say, "62,248 Points as a Valkyrie
   with seed 99999 on a PC at 3.1.2--Beat you by 10,000+ points!"  Or, "It's
   too bad you didn't see Mjollnir sitting in the shop on level 8!")

   What do you think of this idea?  Is it something worth asking the development
   team to look into?  I don't know if it would be easy to implement, as I
   haven't looked at the source code, but I think it might be fun!

Neat idea, but if the usage of random numbers is implemented in
nethack like in any other game I've seen than this unfortunately won't
work. The game uses a stream of random numbers which only depend on
the seed. So if two players have the same seed , they will get the
same stream. Unfortunately that will not lead to the same dungeon for
both players. The same random numbers will get different applications
for different players. Arguably they will have the same first level.
But then maybe one player moves on to the second level immediately and
the second level is created with his current position in the random
number stream. On the other hand, a player which stays a while on the
first level , fight, and in other ways uses up more of the random
numbers will be at another position in the stream when he gets to the
second level. So he will get another second a.s.o. level.

To make your suggestion work you've got to use 2 random number
generators. The first for the typical player-affected things, the
second only for "structural" rolls. (Dungeon shape, initial monster
location, initial treasure location). But even this will have problems
as nethacks doesn't necessarily create levels in sequential order if
you teleport through levels and only later visit them (if I remember
correctly).

Well, this can be fixed , too, by creating a 'random function' f(x,seed). 
Given a certain seed the function f(x) will always produce the same
result. But f(x) itself is random. (Has a 0 auto-correlation in x and seed).
I'm certain someone has somewhere written a paper about suitable
functions of this type.

